    stem: 'A 4-year-old with doll-like facies and hepatomegaly has fasting hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia and hyperlipidemia. Treatment includes frequent cornstarch feeds. The enzyme deficiency is:',
    options: ['Muscle glycogen phosphorylase', 'Lysosomal α-glucosidase', 'Glucose-6-phosphatase', 'Glycogen debranching enzyme'],
    answer: 2,
    explanation: 'Von Gierke disease (Type I) = glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency: the liver cannot release free glucose from G6P → severe fasting hypoglycemia with lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia (G6P shunts to PPP/purines) and hyperlipidemia. Cornstarch provides slow-release glucose. McArdle (V) = muscle phosphorylase (exercise intolerance, no lactate rise); Pompe (II) = lysosomal α-glucosidase (cardiomegaly); Cori (III) = debrancher (milder, normal lactate).',
    teaching: 'Hypoglycemia + lactate + gout + hepatomegaly = Von Gierke (G6Pase).',
